    "note": "Premise: well-posed. A static-lattice 0 K a0 from an E(V) fit of perfect diamond Si is a well-defined quantity; no MD, ensemble or run length is involved, and an 8-atom cubic cell with a periodic MLIP is enough (the worker's 64-atom check matched). Reproduction: I reran the worker's protocol with MACE-MPA-0 medium and got a0 = 5.4665 A, B0 = 87.5 GPa (BM, +/-4%), plus 5.4673 A (+/-2%) and 5.4664 A (Vinet). That matches the reported number to 1e-4 A. Second MLIP: MACE-MP-0 small, a different model trained on MPtrj only, gives a0 = 5.4627-5.4643 A over the same forms and windows. It differs from the worker's value by about 0.003 A (0.06%), far inside the stated +/-0.04 A. Its B0 is softer (71-74 GPa against 87.5), so the bulk modulus depends more on the model than a0 does. Literature anchor: the CODATA 2018 Si lattice parameter, 5.431020511 A at 22.5 C, is quoted correctly in the worker's note. Both MLIPs come out about 0.6% above it, which is the familiar PBE overestimate and is what you expect from PBE-trained universal potentials. The calibration file /data/calibration/tier1.json has no silicon lattice-constant record, so it could not be used here. Caveats: the two MLIPs share PBE training data, so this agreement checks reproducibility and model sensitivity, not accuracy against experiment. The worker already discloses that the value should be read as a ~PBE static-lattice a0 that is biased high by ~0.6%. Their +/-0.04 A total uncertainty covers the experimental value, and their claim that ZPE plus thermal effects shift a0 by only a few 1e-3 A is reasonable for Si. I found no reason to reject the result."
